    "slug": "ai-and-change-management",
    "title": "AI didn't create a new kind of change. It just made everything move faster.",
    "excerpt": "AI rollouts are still change work — identity and trust included — just on a six-week clock instead of six months.",
    "content": "# AI didn't create a new kind of change. It just made everything move faster.\n\nPicture the last \"quick\" software rollout you ran. A copilot bolted onto the CRM, an\nassistant dropped into the ticketing system, a chatbot parked on the intranet. Somewhere\nin the kickoff meeting, someone said the words \"this is really just an IT project,\" and\nyou felt your stomach sink a little, because you already knew how this was going to go.\n\nYou'd be right to worry. And you'd also be the only person in the room who understood\nwhy.\n\n## The tool was never the hard part\n\nHere's the thing nobody in that meeting wants to hear: deploying the software is the easy\n80%. Flip a switch, provision the licenses, send the \"we're live\" email. What's actually\nhard, what's always been hard, is that AI tools ask people to change how they think about\ntheir own competence, not just which button they click. A support agent using an AI\nassistant isn't learning a new field in a form. They're being asked to trust a\nrecommendation they didn't generate themselves, and then to feel confident enough to\noverride it when it's wrong. That's not a training problem. That's an identity problem,\nthe quiet fear of \"does this mean I'm being replaced,\" sitting underneath every \"any\nquestions?\" in the room.\n\nYou already have fifty years of frameworks for exactly this kind of fear. ADKAR, Kotter,\nProsci, none of it wasted, all of it still true. What's changed isn't the psychology.\nIt's the clock. A CRM migration used to hand you six months to build comms and training\naround that fear. An AI rollout hands you six weeks, because the business case was\n\"everyone else is already doing this\" and nobody budgeted for the part where people need\nto feel safe first.\n\n## What doesn't change, even now\n\nYou still need to know who's actually affected, and how badly. You still need comms that\nspeak to one team's specific fear, not a company-wide email that speaks to no one's. You\nstill need to measure whether people actually trust the tool, not just whether they sat\nthrough the training. None of that got easier. It just got less optional, faster.\n\n## Where it actually gets easier\n\nHere's the one genuine gift in all of this: the tools you use to do your own job got\nbetter too. Drafting a comms plan for five different audiences used to eat an afternoon.\nNow the first draft can take minutes, not because the thinking got any less important,\nbut because the blank page stopped being where you lost half your day.\n\nDon't mistake that for permission to skip the thinking. Spend the time you got back\ntalking to the people who are actually going to be affected, the ones with the quiet\nworry in the kickoff meeting. Find out what's really going to make them resist, three\nweeks before it's too late to do anything about it, not three weeks after.\n\nAI rollouts are coming to your desk whether you're ready or not. Treat it like an IT\nticket and you'll have an adoption problem by the next quarterly review. Treat it like\nthe change it actually is, the same way you always have, just faster now, and you'll be\nthe person in the room who saw it coming.",
    "header_image_url": "/blog/ai-and-change-management.jpg",
