    "slug": "state-of-change-management",
    "title": "The state of change management: rising demand, flat headcount, unchanged tooling",
    "excerpt": "Demand is high, headcount has not kept up, and most delivery still runs on spreadsheets from roughly 2010.",
    "content": "# The state of change management: rising demand, flat headcount, unchanged tooling\n\nThree trends are converging on the change management profession right now, and they\ndon't fit together comfortably. Demand for the discipline is at an all-time high.\nStaffing behind it hasn't kept pace. And the software most practitioners rely on to do\nthe actual work has barely shifted in a decade.\n\n## Demand: no longer in question\n\nOrganizations have largely stopped debating whether change management is necessary.\nDigital transformation, AI adoption, restructuring, and M&A activity keep a steady\npipeline of work requiring managed change moving through the market. The open question\nhas shifted from \"do we need this\" to \"who's actually going to deliver it.\"\n\nThat second question is where the strain shows. A decade ago, a large transformation\ntypically justified a dedicated change team led by a certified practitioner with real\nbudget behind them. Today, the same scope of work is increasingly assigned to a single\ninternal generalist, or outsourced to an independent consultant on a fixed fee, expected\nto produce comparable outcomes with a fraction of the resourcing.\n\n## The tooling gap, stated plainly\n\nDespite the discipline's maturity, most of the actual delivery work still happens in\nspreadsheets, slide decks, and email threads. This isn't a knowledge gap among\npractitioners. It's a structural one: the software category was built and priced around\nan older model, one dedicated team running one or two large transformations annually.\nThat model doesn't describe most of the market anymore.\n\nThe result is a profession running mature methodology, ADKAR, Kotter, well-documented\nbest practice, on infrastructure that hasn't meaningfully advanced since roughly 2010.\n\n## Budget scrutiny is reshaping expectations\n\nChange budgets are increasingly absorbed into broader project budgets rather than\nstanding independently, which subjects them to the same scrutiny as any other line item.\nPractitioners are now expected to produce genuine adoption data rather than qualitative\nassurance that a rollout \"went well.\" Anecdote no longer clears a budget review on its\nown.\n\n## What actually differentiates practitioners right now\n\nThe differentiator in this environment isn't certification count, it's capacity: the\nability to run more concurrent change work, across more clients or departments, without\noverhead scaling linearly alongside it. That requires better systems, not longer hours.\nThe methodology hasn't gotten any easier to execute. The expectation to execute it leaner\nhas increased substantially, and the tooling available to meet that expectation is only\nnow beginning to catch up.",
    "header_image_url": "/blog/state-of-change-management.jpg",
    "published": false,
    "display_order": 6
